(6855417, 1031184, 68504, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Giles_uk:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by tnun:</b></div><hr/><i> also like how that blurry dog in the background looks to be the size of a pony. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>That''s the little boys dad...... </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>our joy is complete.</td>', 'Originally posted by Giles_uk: Originally posted by tnun: also like how that blurry dog in the background looks to be the size of a pony.   That''s the little boys dad......   our joy is complete.', '2012-09-03 14:37:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(6855428, 1031061, 142473, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club.<br/> <br/>First, I''m no expert on street photos... but I imagine you intended this photo as a different approach to a street style photo.  Quickly, I''ll touch on why this photo might not have scored so well, then I''ll let you know why I like it.<br/> <br/>It seems people were really looking for an image that told a story.  I think street photos are really about the interaction of people between each other and/or with their environment.  While there is some interpersonal interaction going on here, it''s meaning is not well understood by the viewer.  The man on the left is explaining something, the man on the right is leaning in, while the middle man appears to be watching the photographer.  The columns offer an interesting framing that I like, but I think it was voted down because there wasn''t an intimacy here due to including the columns.<br/> <br/>It has a really interesting and unique feel.  It''s very balanced, very mirrored.  The colors are great.  I personally love blues and yellows.  They seem to complement each other so well.  I also like the gradient of the shirt colors.  White to blue.<br/> <br/>I think it comes down to perception.  As a few people said, the image looks like a screengrab from a 70s film.  Not so much a standard street photo.  But it has a very pleasant artistry to it.  For that reason, and it''s strong composition, I find it very interesting.  Sorry that the majority of voters didn''t agree - it seems the "DNMC" vote (1s and 2s) is what might have got you here, score wise.  Don''t focus on that though, take a look at the numerous good comments.  That''s what means the most.<br/> <br/>Food for thought.<br/> <br/>James Downing</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club. First, I''m no expert on street photos... but I imagine you intended this photo as a different approach to a street style photo.  Quickly, I''ll touch on why this photo might not have scored so well, then I''ll let you know why I like it. It seems people were really looking for an image that told a story.  I think street photos are really about the interaction of people between each other and/or with their environment.  While there is some interpersonal interaction going on here, it''s meaning is not well understood by the viewer.  The man on the left is explaining something, the man on the right is leaning in, while the middle man appears to be watching the photographer.  The columns offer an interesting framing that I like, but I think it was voted down because there wasn''t an intimacy here due to including the columns. It has a really interesting and unique feel.  It''s very balanced, very mirrored.  The colors are great.  I personally love blues and yellows.  They seem to complement each other so well.  I also like the gradient of the shirt colors.  White to blue. I think it comes down to perception.  As a few people said, the image looks like a screengrab from a 70s film.  Not so much a standard street photo.  But it has a very pleasant artistry to it.  For that reason, and it''s strong composition, I find it very interesting.  Sorry that the majority of voters didn''t agree - it seems the "DNMC" vote (1s and 2s) is what might have got you here, score wise.  Don''t focus on that though, take a look at the numerous good comments.  That''s what means the most. Food for thought. James Downing', '2012-09-03 14:46:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(6855482, 1031282, 138630, '<td valign="top">WONDERFUL moment and emotion.  I agree with <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=40391" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">abra</a> and others that a tighter crop taking from right and bw conversion would have been very effective.  Also agree that this was very much under-rated.  I was a 6 but could easily have bumped a lot if it hadn''t felt kind of washed out.</td>', 'WONDERFUL moment and emotion.  I agree with  abra and others that a tighter crop taking from right and bw conversion would have been very effective.  Also agree that this was very much under-rated.  I was a 6 but could easily have bumped a lot if it hadn''t felt kind of washed out.', '2012-09-03 15:43:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(6855486, 1031130, 138630, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by tnun:</b></div><hr/><i> and I like the guy''s head coming out of the woman''s shoulder... </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Actually, that''s another woman, also reading part of a newspaper, seated at the table behind the child.  Thanks so much for the look and the comment :)<br/> <br/><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2012-09-03 14:50:30.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by tnun: and I like the guy''s head coming out of the woman''s shoulder...   Actually, that''s another woman, also reading part of a newspaper, seated at the table behind the child.  Thanks so much for the look and the comment :) ', '2012-09-03 15:48:09.000000', '2012-09-03 15:50:30.000000', 0),
